/**
 * MonotonicStack: A stack that supports O(1) push, pop, and 
 * instant access to the longest increasing subsequence LENGTH
 * ending at the top element. Most useful query beyond min/max!
 */
public class MonotonicStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private static class Node<T> {
        T value;
        int lisLength;  // length of longest increasing subseq ending here
        Node<T> prev;

        Node(T value, int lisLength, Node<T> prev) {
            this.value = value;
            this.lisLength = lisLength;
            this.prev = prev;
        }
    }

    private Node<T> top;

    /**
     * Push a value onto the stack in O(1) time.
     * Updates the LIS length: if value > previous top, 
     * LIS length = previous LIS length + 1; else LIS length = 1.
     */
    public void push(T value) {
        int newLisLength = 1;
        if (top != null && value.compareTo(top.value) > 0) {
            newLisLength = top.lisLength + 1;
        }
        top = new Node<>(value, newLisLength, top);
    }

    /**
     * Pop the top element in O(1) time.
     */
    public T pop() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        T value = top.value;
        top = top.prev;
        return value;
    }

    /**
     * Query: get the length of the longest strictly increasing 
     * subsequence ending at the current top in O(1) time.
     * This tells you "how long a streak of growth are we in?"
     */
    public int getLisLength() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return top.lisLength;
    }

    /**
     * Bonus: peek at top without popping, O(1).
     */
    public T peek() {
        if (top == null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return top.value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return top == null;
    }
}
```

**Why LIS length?** Min/max are overdone. LIS length is genuinely useful—it answers "are we in an uptrend?" and appears in dynamic programming problems. Each node just stores one integer tracking the streak, computed instantly from the previous node's state. Push/pop/query all touch exactly one node pointer and do O(1) work.

