public class MaxStack<T extends Comparable<T>> {
    private static class Node<T> {
        T value;
        T maxSoFar;  // Maximum from bottom up to this node

        Node(T value, T maxSoFar) {
            this.value = value;
            this.maxSoFar = maxSoFar;
        }
    }

    private LinkedList<Node<T>> stack = new LinkedList<>();

    /**
     * Push a value onto the stack. O(1).
     */
    public void push(T value) {
        T newMax;
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            newMax = value;
        } else {
            T currentMax = stack.peek().maxSoFar;
            newMax = value.compareTo(currentMax) > 0 ? value : currentMax;
        }
        stack.push(new Node<>(value, newMax));
    }

    /**
     * Pop and return the top value. O(1).
     */
    public T pop() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.pop().value;
    }

    /**
     * Query the maximum value currently in the stack. O(1).
     */
    public T getMax() {
        if (stack.isEmpty()) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack.peek().maxSoFar;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public int size() {
        return stack.size();
    }
}
```

**Why this feature?** Max-query is common in competitive algorithms (sliding window problems, priority tracking) and the technique—caching aggregate state at each node—generalizes to min, sum, or even median approximations.

